Issue number 2 is simple to reproduce, you only need one tank. Just need to force fire slightly out of range so that the tank moves into onto the cell and stops to start firing. Once it is firing, issue a stop order and it's stopped on the space.

Issue is that attacking uses `MoveWithinRange`, which is just a subclass of `MoveAdjacentTo` that stops when it's within range. `MoveAdjacentTo` actually cancels the `Move` order as soon as it is within range (as defined by `MoveWithinRange`), but `Move` simply avoids clearing its path:
```C#
		public override void Cancel(Actor self, bool keepQueue = false)
		{
			// We need to clear the path here in order to prevent MovePart queueing new instances of itself
			// when the unit is making a turn.
			if (path != null && mobile.CanStayInCell(mobile.ToCell))
				path.Clear();

			base.Cancel(self, keepQueue);
		}
```
The path is actually already empty here, so the unit stops on the cell that it is not meant to stay on. The move order is then successfully finished because either its path count is zero or it's at its destination (in this case, both):
```C#
		public override bool Tick(Actor self)
		{
			// ...
			if (IsCanceling && mobile.CanStayInCell(mobile.ToCell))
			{
				if (path != null)
					path.Clear();

				return true;
			}

			// ...

			if (destination == mobile.ToCell)
				return true;

			if (path.Count == 0)
			{
				destination = mobile.ToCell;
				return false;
			}

```

`MoveAdjacentTo` is used **directly** by `Enter`, one of the only places to use it, debugging that now.

>  @tovl do you have any thoughts on simpler solutions here?

Yes. The second issue is solved by adding the following to `Mobile.OnBecomingIdle`:

```
if (!Locomotor.CanStayInCell(self.Location))
{
     self.QueueActivity(MoveTo(self.Location, evaluateNearestMovableCell: true));
     return;
}
```
This then also makes it redundant to make the cancelling of `Move` conditional on `CanStayInCell`. Removing that condition mostly solves the first issue as well. The units will still wait on a cell they shouldn't, but they won't be uncontrollable.

The best way to make sure units won't want to wait for their turn on the repair pad in those cells in the first place is to actually implement the concept of waiting for their turn, i.e. to implement reservation logic for repair pads. The lack of that doesn't strike me as a release-blocking issue though, as long as the issue of those units becoming uncontrollable is fixed.

> the idea that certain moves can stop on a transit-only cell is still a potential problem (easy to do with force-fire).

This can/should be fixed by making `MoveAdjacentTo` and `MoveWithinRange` understand that they are not valid stopping locations:
```diff
diff --git a/OpenRA.Mods.Common/Activities/Move/MoveAdjacentTo.cs b/OpenRA.Mods.Common/Activities/Move/MoveAdjacentTo.cs
index 7f83636d37..6561f8a15d 100644
--- a/OpenRA.Mods.Common/Activities/Move/MoveAdjacentTo.cs
+++ b/OpenRA.Mods.Common/Activities/Move/MoveAdjacentTo.cs
@@ -78,7 +78,8 @@ protected virtual bool ShouldRepath(Actor self, CPos targetLocation)
 
                protected virtual IEnumerable<CPos> CandidateMovementCells(Actor self)
                {
-                       return Util.AdjacentCells(self.World, Target);
+                       return Util.AdjacentCells(self.World, Target)
+                               .Where(c => Mobile.CanStayInCell(c));
                }
 
                protected override void OnFirstRun(Actor self)
diff --git a/OpenRA.Mods.Common/Activities/Move/MoveWithinRange.cs b/OpenRA.Mods.Common/Activities/Move/MoveWithinRange.cs
index 0dfae9694d..c79ac68951 100644
--- a/OpenRA.Mods.Common/Activities/Move/MoveWithinRange.cs
+++ b/OpenRA.Mods.Common/Activities/Move/MoveWithinRange.cs
@@ -33,13 +33,13 @@ protected override bool ShouldStop(Actor self)
                {
                        // We are now in range. Don't move any further!
                        // HACK: This works around the pathfinder not returning the shortest path
-                       return AtCorrectRange(self.CenterPosition) && Mobile.CanInteractWithGroundLayer(self);
+                       return AtCorrectRange(self.CenterPosition) && Mobile.CanInteractWithGroundLayer(self) && Mobile.CanStayInCell(self.Location);
                }
 
                protected override bool ShouldRepath(Actor self, CPos targetLocation)
                {
                        return lastVisibleTargetLocation != targetLocation && (!AtCorrectRange(self.CenterPosition)
-                               || !Mobile.CanInteractWithGroundLayer(self));
+                               || !Mobile.CanInteractWithGroundLayer(self) || !Mobile.CanStayInCell(self.Location));
                }
 
                protected override IEnumerable<CPos> CandidateMovementCells(Actor self)
@@ -49,7 +49,7 @@ protected override IEnumerable<CPos> CandidateMovementCells(Actor self)
                        var minCells = minRange.Length / 1024;
 
                        return map.FindTilesInAnnulus(lastVisibleTargetLocation, minCells, maxCells)
-                               .Where(c => AtCorrectRange(map.CenterOfSubCell(c, Mobile.FromSubCell)));
+                               .Where(c => Mobile.CanStayInCell(c) && AtCorrectRange(map.CenterOfSubCell(c, Mobile.FromSubCell)));
                }
 
                bool AtCorrectRange(WPos origin)
```

As a side note, we should also fix the code style issue of `CanStayInCell` not having an initial `self` parameter.

<hr>

> This then also makes it redundant to make the cancelling of `Move` conditional on `CanStayInCell`. 

This risks regressing any cases where `Move` is a child activity that is cancelled by a parent without the unit going idle.

```C#
              protected virtual IEnumerable<CPos> CandidateMovementCells(Actor self)
                {
-                       return Util.AdjacentCells(self.World, Target);
+                       return Util.AdjacentCells(self.World, Target)
+                               .Where(c => Mobile.CanStayInCell(c));
                }
```

@pchote This method will break in the case that a target is surrounded by transit-only tiles. No tiles will be returned and `MoveAdjacentTo` will be unable to calculate a path at all, which I'm not sure is always the desired behaviour.
```C#
	foreach (var cell in CandidateMovementCells(self))
					if (domainIndex.IsPassable(loc, cell, Mobile.Info.LocomotorInfo) && Mobile.CanEnterCell(cell))
						searchCells.Add(cell);
			}

			if (!searchCells.Any())
				return NoPath;
```

I don't think targets being surrounded with transit-only is technically possible with structure layouts right now? So in practice it probably works fine.

